# Crash dumps and profiles. This one is not boilerplate: the suite deliberately
# faults on guard pages, and this library's entire purpose is keeping secret
# pages out of exactly these artifacts. A core file committed to a public repo
# for a memory-hardening library would be a uniquely bad look, and the default
# Linux core pattern drops it in the working directory.
#
# /core is root-anchored, and !/core/ re-includes a directory of that name — the
# anchor alone still matches a top-level core/ package, which would silently
# vanish from a future commit.
#
# The dotted forms cover what kernel.core_pattern actually produces: bare "core"
# is the classic default, %p gives core.<pid>, and the common
# "core.%e.%p.%t" gives core.<exe>.<pid>.<time>. Matching only a leading digit
# after the dot misses every pattern that puts the executable name first. Not
# using a bare core.* because that would swallow core.go.
/core
!/core/
core.[0-9]*
core.*.[0-9]*
*.dmp
*.stackdump
*.pprof
hs_err_pid*.log
__debug_bin*

